# Sign JWS and encrypt JWE envelopes

This notebook signs a compact JWS ([RFC 7515](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7515.html)), encrypts it inside a compact JWE ([RFC 7516](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7516.html)), and verifies both layers before reading the payload. It then decrypts with an RSA-OAEP recipient key held behind a handle and shows the bounded errors. Keys are generated locally; no network access is needed beyond the Hex install.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:request_seal, "~> 0.5.0"}])
```

## Sign and verify a JWS

The payload is a sensor reading. `{:jws, "EdDSA"}` binds the handle to the JOSE algorithm name, which is separate from the HTTP signature name `"ed25519"`.

```elixir
reading = ~s({"sensor":"greenhouse-3","temperature_c":21.4})

{_public_bytes, seed} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
{:ok, signing_handle} = RequestSeal.Custody.Local.new({:jws, "EdDSA"}, {:ed25519, seed})
{:ok, signing_key} = RequestSeal.Custody.public_key(signing_handle)

{:ok, token} =
  RequestSeal.JOSE.JWS.sign([{"alg", "EdDSA"}, {"typ", "JOSE"}], reading, signing_handle)

jws_policy = %{
  algorithms: ["EdDSA"],
  timeout: 5_000,
  key_resolver: fn %{algorithm: "EdDSA"} ->
    {:ok, %{algorithm: "EdDSA", key: signing_key}}
  end
}

{:ok, jws} = RequestSeal.JOSE.JWS.verify(token, jws_policy)
^reading = jws.payload
%{"alg" => "EdDSA", "typ" => "JOSE"} = jws.header
3 = length(String.split(token, "."))
IO.puts("JWS verified: #{byte_size(token)} compact bytes")
```

The resolver supplies the trusted key; a `kid` or `x5c` header alone confers no trust.

## Reject a changed JWS

```elixir
[protected, _payload, signature] = String.split(token, ".")
forged_payload = Base.url_encode64(~s({"sensor":"greenhouse-3","temperature_c":35.0}), padding: false)

{:error, %{reason: :invalid_signature, layer: :crypto}} =
  RequestSeal.JOSE.JWS.verify(Enum.join([protected, forged_payload, signature], "."), jws_policy)

# An algorithm outside the policy's allowlist rejects.
{:error, %{reason: :algorithm_not_permitted}} =
  RequestSeal.JOSE.JWS.verify(token, %{jws_policy | algorithms: ["ES256"]})

IO.puts("Changed payload and unlisted algorithm rejected")
```

## Encrypt the signed token

The JWE wraps a fresh content-encryption key with an AES key you hold. The wrapping key never leaves your callbacks.

```elixir
wrapping_key = {:aes, :crypto.strong_rand_bytes(32)}

wrap = fn algorithm, cek ->
  RequestSeal.JOSE.KeyManagement.wrap(algorithm, cek, %{}, wrapping_key)
end

{:ok, encrypted} =
  RequestSeal.JOSE.JWE.encrypt(
    [{"alg", "A256GCMKW"}, {"enc", "A256GCM"}, {"cty", "JWS"}],
    token,
    wrap
  )

5 = length(String.split(encrypted, "."))
IO.puts("JWE produced: #{byte_size(encrypted)} compact bytes")
```

## Decrypt and verify both layers

`RequestSeal.JOSE.Nested.verify/4` decrypts the JWE, checks its declared content type, then verifies the inner JWS. It returns both results only when every step succeeds.

```elixir
jwe_policy = %{
  algorithms: ["A256GCMKW"],
  encryption: ["A256GCM"],
  max_plaintext: 1_048_576,
  timeout: 5_000,
  key_resolver: fn %{algorithm: "A256GCMKW"} ->
    {:ok,
     %{
       algorithm: "A256GCMKW",
       unwrap: fn bytes, header ->
         RequestSeal.JOSE.KeyManagement.unwrap("A256GCMKW", bytes, header, wrapping_key)
       end
     }}
  end
}

{:ok, %{jwe: outer, jws: inner}} =
  RequestSeal.JOSE.Nested.verify(encrypted, jwe_policy, jws_policy, content_types: ["JWS"])

^token = outer.plaintext
^reading = inner.payload

# A ciphertext byte changed in transit fails authentication without releasing plaintext.
[h, k, iv, ciphertext, tag] = String.split(encrypted, ".")
<<first, rest::binary>> = Base.url_decode64!(ciphertext, padding: false)
flipped = Base.url_encode64(<<Bitwise.bxor(first, 1), rest::binary>>, padding: false)

{:error, %{reason: :decryption_failed, layer: :crypto}} =
  RequestSeal.JOSE.JWE.decrypt(Enum.join([h, k, iv, flipped, tag], "."), jwe_policy)

IO.puts("Nested envelope verified; altered ciphertext rejected")
```

Successful decryption shows the envelope was made with the shared wrapping key; it does not identify who made it. The inner signature identifies the signing key.

## Decrypt with a recipient key handle

For RSA-OAEP, the recipient's private key lives in a handle bound to `{:jwe, "RSA-OAEP-256"}`. Senders encrypt to its public key; the resolver returns the handle.

```elixir
private = :public_key.generate_key({:rsa, 2048, 65537})
pem = :public_key.pem_encode([:public_key.pem_entry_encode(:RSAPrivateKey, private)])

{:ok, recipient} = RequestSeal.Custody.Local.import({:jwe, "RSA-OAEP-256"}, pem, :pem)
{:ok, recipient_public} = RequestSeal.Custody.public_key(recipient)

{:ok, sealed_note} =
  RequestSeal.JOSE.JWE.encrypt(
    [{"alg", "RSA-OAEP-256"}, {"enc", "A256GCM"}],
    "rotate the staging certificate on Friday",
    recipient_public
  )

recipient_policy = %{
  algorithms: ["RSA-OAEP-256"],
  encryption: ["A256GCM"],
  max_plaintext: 1_048_576,
  timeout: 5_000,
  key_resolver: fn %{algorithm: "RSA-OAEP-256"} ->
    {:ok, %{algorithm: "RSA-OAEP-256", key: recipient}}
  end
}

{:ok, opened} = RequestSeal.JOSE.JWE.decrypt(sealed_note, recipient_policy)
"rotate the staging certificate on Friday" = opened.plaintext

# After release, decryption fails with the same error as a forged message.
:ok = RequestSeal.Custody.Local.release(recipient)
{:error, %{reason: :decryption_failed}} = RequestSeal.JOSE.JWE.decrypt(sealed_note, recipient_policy)

# The public-key health check tells the two cases apart.
{:error, %{reason: :key_not_found}} = RequestSeal.Custody.public_key(recipient)

:ok = RequestSeal.Custody.Local.release(signing_handle)
IO.puts("Recipient handle decrypted; released handle reported separately")
```

Next: the [JOSE guide](../docs/guides/jose.md) for options, bounds, and the full error list; [key custody](../docs/guides/key-custody.md) for supervised key owners.
